\" \"$@\") >\"$stdout_file\" 2>\"$stderr_file\"; then\n    status=0\n  else\n    status=$?\n  fi\n  if [ \"$status\" -ne \"$expected_exit\" ]; then\n    printf 'unexpected exit code: expected %s got %s\\n' \"$expected_exit\" \"$status\" >&2\n    printf 'stdout:\\n' >&2\n    cat \"$stdout_file\" >&2\n    printf 'stderr:\\n' >&2\n    cat \"$stderr_file\" >&2\n    rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  if [ -n \"$expected_stdout\" ]; then\n    grep -Fq \"$expected_stdout\" \"$stdout_file\" || {\n      printf 'expected stdout to contain: %s\\n' \"$expected_stdout\" >&2\n      printf 'stdout:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stdout_file\" >&2\n      printf 'stderr:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stderr_file\" >&2\n      rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n      exit 1\n    }\n  fi\n  if [ -n \"$expected_stderr\" ]; then\n    grep -Fq \"$expected_stderr\" \"$stderr_file\" || {\n      printf 'expected stderr to contain: %s\\n' \"$expected_stderr\" >&2\n      printf 'stdout:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stdout_file\" >&2\n      printf 'stderr:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stderr_file\" >&2\n      rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n      exit 1\n    }\n  fi\n  rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n}\n\nrun_pr_case() {\n  local repo_dir=\"$1\"\n  local fake_gh_dir=\"$2\"\n  local expected_exit=\"$3\"\n  local expected_stdout=\"$4\"\n  local expected_stderr=\"$5\"\n  local branch_protection_log=\"${6:-docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md}\"\n  local stdout_file stderr_file status\n  stdout_file=\"$(mktemp \"${TMPDIR}/stdout.XXXXXX\")\"\n  stderr_file=\"$(mktemp \"${TMPDIR}/stderr.XXXXXX\")\"\n  if (\n    cd \"$repo_dir\" &&\n    PATH=\"$fake_gh_dir:$PATH\" \\\n    \"$SCRIPT\" --pr 99 --dispatch-note DISPATCH-NOTE.md --status-md STATUS.md --branch-protection-log \"$branch_protection_log\"\n  ) >\"$stdout_file\" 2>\"$stderr_file\"; then\n    status=0\n  else\n    status=$?\n  fi\n  if [ \"$status\" -ne \"$expected_exit\" ]; then\n    printf 'unexpected exit code: expected %s got %s\\n' \"$expected_exit\" \"$status\" >&2\n    printf 'stdout:\\n' >&2\n    cat \"$stdout_file\" >&2\n    printf 'stderr:\\n' >&2\n    cat \"$stderr_file\" >&2\n    rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  if [ -n \"$expected_stdout\" ]; then\n    grep -Fq \"$expected_stdout\" \"$stdout_file\" || {\n      printf 'expected stdout to contain: %s\\n' \"$expected_stdout\" >&2\n      printf 'stdout:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stdout_file\" >&2\n      printf 'stderr:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stderr_file\" >&2\n      rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n      exit 1\n    }\n  fi\n  if [ -n \"$expected_stderr\" ]; then\n    grep -Fq \"$expected_stderr\" \"$stderr_file\" || {\n      printf 'expected stderr to contain: %s\\n' \"$expected_stderr\" >&2\n      printf 'stdout:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stdout_file\" >&2\n      printf 'stderr:\\n' >&2\n      cat \"$stderr_file\" >&2\n      rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n      exit 1\n    }\n  fi\n  rm -f \"$stdout_file\" \"$stderr_file\"\n}\n\nmain() {\n  local repo_dir\n\n  grep -Eq 'not applicable.*tooling slices ONLY|tooling slices.*ONLY' \"$SCRIPT\" || fail \"script missing not-applicable tooling-slice documentation\"\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case1\"\n  init_case_repo \"$repo_dir\"\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/DISPATCH-NOTE.md\" <<'EOF'\ncascade-status: onboarded\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\nEOF\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\n---\n\n## Invocation log entry\n\n~~~markdown\n### 2026-05-09T00:00:00Z — jrnb2024/pim@main\n\n- **Operator:** @tester\n~~~\nEOF\n  commit_case \"$repo_dir\" \"case 1\"\n  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 0 'OK: cascade-status=onboarded; 2 checks passed' ''\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case2\"\n  init_case_repo \"$repo_dir\"\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/DISPATCH-NOTE.md\" <<'EOF'\ncascade-status: onboarded\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\nEOF\n  commit_case \"$repo_dir\" \"case 2\"\n  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 1 '' 'ERROR: branch-protection-log.md was not modified for cascade-status=onboarded'\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case3\"\n  init_case_repo \"$repo_dir\"\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/DISPATCH-NOTE.md\" <<'EOF'\ncascade-status: onboarded\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\nEOF\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\n---\n\n## Invocation log entry\n\n~~~markdown\n### 2026-05-09T00:00:00Z — jrnb2024/wrong@main\n\n- **Operator:** @tester\n~~~\nEOF\n  commit_case \"$repo_dir\" \"case 3\"\n  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 1 '' \"ERROR: log entry target 'jrnb2024/wrong' does not match DISPATCH-NOTE target 'jrnb2024/pim'\"\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case3b\"\n  init_case_repo \"$repo_dir\"\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/DISPATCH-NOTE.md\" <<'EOF'\ncascade-status: onboarded\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/wrong\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\nEOF\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\n---\n\n## Invocation log entry\n\n~~~markdown\n### 2026-05-09T00:00:00Z — jrnb2024/wrong@main\n\n- **Operator:** @tester\n~~~\nEOF\n  commit_case \"$repo_dir\" \"case 3b\"\n  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 1 '' 'ERROR: expected exactly one **Target:** match in DISPATCH-NOTE, found 2: jrnb2024/wrong, jrnb2024/pim'\n\n  # Plan-doc §2 invariant 2 worked-example matrix plus the operator-bump\n  # unmodified-log negative guard.\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case4a\"\n  init_case_repo \"$repo_dir\"\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/DISPATCH-NOTE.md\" <<'EOF'\ncascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\nEOF\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/STATUS.md\" <<'EOF'\n- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim** (open): Renovate disabled on PIM; operator-bumped @abc123 at 024C; track until adopter enables Renovate cohort.\nEOF\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\n---\n\n## Invocation log entry\n\n~~~markdown\n### 2026-05-09T00:00:00Z — jrnb2024/pim@main\n\n- **Operator:** @tester\n~~~\nEOF\n  commit_case \"$repo_dir\" \"case 4a\"\n  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 0 'OK: cascade-status=onboarded-operator-bump; 3 checks passed' ''\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case4a-negative\"\n  init_case_repo \"$repo_dir\"\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/DISPATCH-NOTE.md\" <<'EOF'\ncascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump\n- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\nEOF\n  cat >\"$repo_dir/STATUS.md\" <<'EOF'\n- **TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-pim** (open): Renovate disabled on PIM; operator-bumped @abc123 at 024C; track until adopter enables Renovate cohort.\nEOF\n  commit_case \"$repo_dir\" \"case 4a-negative\"\n  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 1 '' 'ERROR: branch-protection-log.md was not modified for cascade-status=onboarded-operator-bump'\n\n  repo_dir=\"$TMPDIR/case4a-target-mismatch\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \"D-044|D-039|D-040|limitation-note|SAFE-MAJ-001\" docs/DECISIONS.md'","aggregated_output":"3:**Last Updated:** 2026-05-10 (D-044 filed for WP-SCP-024 024B-core; estate cascade scope split; 024B-core fix-round-12 closure note)\n29:> **D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 024A):**\n30:> D-044 was reserved for WP-SCP-024 slice 024B (`scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh`\n44:> assign D-044, D-045, or D-046 to any other decision filed during that\n84:| D-036 | 2026-05-01 | Adopt `policies/VERSIONING.md` MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH semver contract on the SCP federation primitive's public surfaces (`workflow_call.inputs` on `policy-check.yml`, `schemas/policy-check-summary.schema.json` + `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`, `SCP-R-NNN` rule IDs) WITH a one-release deprecation ramp before any breaking change AND `docs/reviews/rule-proposals/` RFC-lite process (quorum=1 SCP-CODEOWNER per D-031 single-operator mode; 48h wall-clock window with weekend-counts; 0 approvals = auto-defer with `defer` label; bypass-introducing proposals require explicit §5 enumeration and a non-waivable 48h window per 020H.1 R1 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure) as estate doctrine for SCP federation primitive versioning and rule governance. Closes WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 (i)+(ii)+(iii) and BS-5. Forward-compat to 2-of-N quorum when a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation path, 2026-07-21 review). Internal-only surfaces (`lib/policy_check_invocation.sh`, `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`, helper scripts in `scripts/`, Renovate preset internal shape, OPA Rego helpers in `policies/scp_common.rego`) are explicitly NOT covered — they may be refactored without notice | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-035 (020G adopter-helper invocation): every new governance procedure binding adopter behaviour ships a ratifying D-NNN row to make the doctrine searchable in the canonical decision-record home rather than buried in a slice DISPATCH-NOTE. The one-release deprecation ramp is the MINIMUM version-skew adopters must tolerate before a surface vanishes; combined with the Renovate preset's automated SHA-pin bumps, this gives every adopter at least one full PR cycle's worth of warning annotations before a breaking change lands. The RFC-lite process is intentionally lightweight at v1.0.0 because the SCP estate is single-operator; the auto-defer mechanism is currently a manual close + label step. As estate scale grows (WP-SCP-024 cascade), the auto-defer mechanism may be lifted to a GitHub Action that runs on a schedule. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 plan row + 020H.1 R1 completeness COMP-MAJ-003 closure |\n85:| D-037 | 2026-05-01 | Grant `issues: write` permission to the `release-gate-violation-issue` job in `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` (per WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1, closing TF-020H3rg-003). Privilege scope is the JOB level (not workflow default); the release-gate evaluation job continues to run with `contents: read` only. The new job fires only on `needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'` — workflow_dispatch dry-runs and clean push:tags runs never reach it. The job's only mutation is `gh issue create` / `gh issue comment` / `gh label create --force` against the SCP source repo's own Issues namespace; no other write surface is unlocked. The auto-opened issue assigns `@jrnb2024` (single-operator mode per D-031); forward-compat env `SCP_RELEASE_GATE_ASSIGNEES` for 2nd-maintainer escalation. Symmetric with D-029 (020C.1 `statuses: write` for read-back commit-status) — a privilege-write expansion ratified by an amending decision row that names the precise threat-surface and bounded behaviour | ACCEPTED | The release-gate's last-line-of-defense alarm needs SOME write surface to surface beyond the workflow log; opening a GitHub issue is the lightest-weight signal that survives operator absence (the workflow log is ephemeral; the issue persists + assigns + sends a notification). Privilege-of-least-privilege is honoured by job-level scoping rather than workflow-level — the gate-evaluation steps (pip install, schema validate, ramp check, expired-config check) cannot accidentally open issues because they run in the release-gate job which has only `contents: read`. CODEOWNERS coverage on `.github/**` (per WP-SCP-020 020K) gates any change to release-gate.yml itself. The forward-compat env mirrors `SCP_REGRESSION_ASSIGNEES` from canary-replay.yml. Identified at WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 + SAFE-MAJ-001 closures |\n87:| D-039 | 2026-05-02 | Extend the slice 020M hash-pinned Python supply-chain install pattern to the SCP-self conflict-gate workflow (`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` \"Install Python dependencies\" step) per WP-SCP-022 slice 020N, closing TF-020M-001. New `requirements/conflict-gate.in` (top-level pins matching `pyproject.toml` `[project.dependencies]` + `[project.optional-dependencies.dev]`: fastapi, uvicorn, httpx, jsonschema, pydantic, pyyaml, pytest, ruff, mypy) + generated `requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (pip-compile --generate-hashes under Python 3.12 to match `setup-python: python-version: \"3.12\"`; full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s). Install pattern: (i) `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (PyPI deps); (ii) `pip install --no-deps vendor/python/ct_auth-0.8.0-py3-none-any.whl` (local vendored wheel — not on PyPI; CODEOWNERS-protected via `vendor/** @jrnb2024`); (iii) `pip install --no-deps -e .` (editable local SCP package without re-resolving deps already hash-verified in step (i)); (iv) post-install version-pin assertion (`assert yaml.__version__ == \"6.0.3\"; assert jsonschema.__version__ == \"4.26.0\"; assert fastapi.__version__ == \"0.136.1\"; assert pydantic.VERSION == \"2.13.3\"`) defending against future-PR drift (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). Deliberate pyyaml-version drift accepted: conflict-gate.txt resolves pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n88:> D-044 limitation note: the CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient. 024B-extras must hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows; that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001).\n90:| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS` member; there are no external reviewers for the wall-clock pause to enable. The recursive 3-lens R1/R2 adversarial review (per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`) IS the adversarial-review surface; the wall-clock pause was vestigial theatre that produced no additional safety. The operator MAY merge a proposal as soon as adversarial review reaches fixpoint (no new CRIT/MAJ on a complete cycle) AND the proposal PR is open with the `scp-rule-proposal` label applied. The 48h CEILING remains in force: zero approvals by 48h still triggers auto-defer (`defer` label per slice 020L label-provisioning). When a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), the 48h becomes a mandatory MINIMUM to give the second maintainer time to review — the CEILING-not-FLOOR posture is single-operator-only. Bypass-introducing proposals (`Bypass-surface non-empty: true`) retain their non-waivable 48h MINIMUM regardless of operator-count posture, because the additional adversarial-review value of a wall-clock pause IS material when a new bypass surface is being introduced (the operator may catch their own bypass-introducing PR's flaws on second look more reliably than for a non-bypass additive rule). Surfaced by 020L dogfood (the first RFC actually walked through the process). Closes WP-SCP-022 020L post-R2 process-gap | ACCEPTED | The 020L dogfood surfaced this gap operationally: the operator (single-maintainer) reached R1+R2 fixpoint on a non-bypass proposal and was about to wait 48h before merging — at which point the wait was identified as theatre rather than safety (no second pair of eyes to chime in). Theatre-vs-safety is the doctrine question the dogfood is designed to surface cheaply. The amendment narrows the discretionary posture to single-operator + non-bypass + post-fixpoint, leaving every other shape of the 48h window unchanged: bypass-introducing proposals keep the non-waivable minimum (the threat-surface justifies the wait); the post-2nd-maintainer-onboard 48h becomes mandatory minimum so the second maintainer has time to review; the 48h CEILING for auto-defer remains so abandoned proposals are surfaced. Symmetric with D-033's \"operational reality must amend the spec when the spec encounters a constraint that wasn't anticipated\" pattern. Identified at WP-SCP-022 slice 020L post-R2 fixpoint by operator (`@jrnb2024`) when reviewing the merge-readiness of PR #89 |\n94:| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
