 pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n    88\t> D-044 limitation note: the CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient. 024B-extras must hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows; that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001).\n    89\t\n    90\t| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS` member; there are no external reviewers for the wall-clock pause to enable. The recursive 3-lens R1/R2 adversarial review (per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`) IS the adversarial-review surface; the wall-clock pause was vestigial theatre that produced no additional safety. The operator MAY merge a proposal as soon as adversarial review reaches fixpoint (no new CRIT/MAJ on a complete cycle) AND the proposal PR is open with the `scp-rule-proposal` label applied. The 48h CEILING remains in force: zero approvals by 48h still triggers auto-defer (`defer` label per slice 020L label-provisioning). When a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), the 48h becomes a mandatory MINIMUM to give the second maintainer time to review — the CEILING-not-FLOOR posture is single-operator-only. Bypass-introducing proposals (`Bypass-surface non-empty: true`) retain their non-waivable 48h MINIMUM regardless of operator-count posture, because the additional adversarial-review value of a wall-clock pause IS material when a new bypass surface is being introduced (the operator may catch their own bypass-introducing PR's flaws on second look more reliably than for a non-bypass additive rule). Surfaced by 020L dogfood (the first RFC actually walked through the process). Closes WP-SCP-022 020L post-R2 process-gap | ACCEPTED | The 020L dogfood surfaced this gap operationally: the operator (single-maintainer) reached R1+R2 fixpoint on a non-bypass proposal and was about to wait 48h before merging — at which point the wait was identified as theatre rather than safety (no second pair of eyes to chime in). Theatre-vs-safety is the doctrine question the dogfood is designed to surface cheaply. The amendment narrows the discretionary posture to single-operator + non-bypass + post-fixpoint, leaving every other shape of the 48h window unchanged: bypass-introducing proposals keep the non-waivable minimum (the threat-surface justifies the wait); the post-2nd-maintainer-onboard 48h becomes mandatory minimum so the second maintainer has time to review; the 48h CEILING for auto-defer remains so abandoned proposals are surfaced. Symmetric with D-033's \"operational reality must amend the spec when the spec encounters a constraint that wasn't anticipated\" pattern. Identified at WP-SCP-022 slice 020L post-R2 fixpoint by operator (`@jrnb2024`) when reviewing the merge-readiness of PR #89 |\n    91\t| D-041 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B (this PR). The schema defines aggregated per-rule counts (raw_findings, denies, waived, rule_config_disabled), waivers aggregate (active_count, by_rule_id, expiring_within_30d), rule-config aggregate (disabled_rules, expiring_within_30d), repo / ref / commit / scp_version / RFC3339 emitted_at, and a verdict (allow/deny/warn). The schema EXPLICITLY DOES NOT include waiver content (`reason`, `approved_by`, `waiver_id` strings) — invariant 2 of WP-SCP-023 plan-doc binds this. The reusable workflow gains a `scorecard-emit: bool` `workflow_call` input (default false) so existing adopters see no behaviour change; opt-in adopters set `with: scorecard-emit: true`. Schema versioning: MAJOR-pinned per `policies/VERSIONING.md` D-036 + WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1 — aggregator drops emits with unknown `schema_version`. Verification: GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation (OIDC-based) via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0` (SHA `a2bbfa25...`) at emit time; aggregator verifies via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow` (per D-042 to be filed at slice 023C). v1.2.0 cut on this slice's merge per VERSIONING.md MINOR category | ACCEPTED | This is the first slice of WP-SCP-023's implementation chain (023A plan-doc landed at PR #97, commit `7075234`, 2026-05-03 R4 fixpoint). The data shape was specified at plan-doc level; this slice ratifies it as code surface. Default-false on the workflow input is the load-bearing safety property: existing adopters cannot have new behaviour spring on them; opt-in is explicit per invariant 4. The waiver-content exclusion (invariant 2) is enforced by the schema's `additionalProperties: false` constraint on every level + the explicit field enumeration. The MAJOR-pinning posture means schema breakage requires v2.0.0 + adopter migration; v0.1 prefix on `schema_version` reflects the freedom to amend until first adopter opts in. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation |\n    92\t| D-042 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C (this PR). Mechanism: per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (the GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation OIDC pattern, mandatory per the D-042 reservation note above + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits (slice 020J) + `output/scorecards/** @jrnb2024` CODEOWNERS coverage (slice 023A) + branch protection on main (slice 020D2), NOT a separate Ed25519 key layer. The aggregator runs as `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` on a weekly cron (Monday 06:00 UTC) + `workflow_dispatch`; two-job structure (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped via job-level permissions, mirroring the 023B `attest-scorecard` job split); reads `docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml` (CODEOWNERS-protected at `docs/scorecards/**`), pulls each adopter's latest green policy-check run's `scorecard-emit.json` artifact, verifies attestation, schema-validates emit, aggregates into `output/scorecards/index.json` (schema: `schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`), and opens a PR for operator review. Failure modes (verification_failure / unreachable / no_emit) record loudly per-adopter — never silently dropped. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema `additionalProperties: false` at every level). Internal-only workflow per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\" (mirrors 020N conflict-gate.yml posture); no semver bump. Verifies WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 6 (\"Aggregator output is Ed25519-signed\") in spirit — Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub rather than by an SCP-side private key, eliminating the private-key-distribution surface | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 (per-emit data shape + opt-in model) — D-041 ratified the EMITTER side of the trust model; D-042 ratifies the AGGREGATOR / VERIFIER side. The mandatory `--signer-workflow` constraint was already pre-committed at the D-042 reservation note above (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001) — this slice ratifies it as workflow code. The Ed25519-via-Sigstore framing eliminates the chicken-and-egg of \"where does the SCP private key live and how does the aggregator hold it without exposing adopters\" — GitHub manages the keys via OIDC + Sigstore. The two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) is identical to 023B's `policy-check` + `attest-scorecard` split (closes the analogous threat surface where workflow-level write permissions could be exploited by a compromised aggregate-side step). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure |\n    93\t| D-043 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report (`docs/scorecards/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`) + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D (this PR). Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` produces deterministic golden-file-tested output reading `output/scorecards/index.json`; aggregator workflow `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` extended in the commit job to invoke the generator + add the rendered markdown to the operator-review PR alongside the index. MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` (registered in `src/standards_control_plane/mcp_server/tools.py`): read-only contract per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 5; request shape `{repo_filter?, since_emitted_at?}`; response shape excludes waiver content (`reason` / `approved_by` / `waiver_id` — invariant 2) by Pydantic model construction (extra=forbid + explicit field whitelist). The MCP method reads `output/scorecards/index.json` from the SCP repo working copy at call time; staleness is observable via the response's `aggregated_at` field. ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by Pydantic models + schema + dedicated pytest scans on both markdown + MCP responses) | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 + D-042 — D-043 ratifies the CONSUMER side: markdown for operators/auditors, MCP for agents/tooling. Read-only contract + Pydantic-enforced field whitelist + golden-file pytest together preserve invariant 2 across both surfaces. The MCP method does NOT introduce a new authentication primitive — it rides on WP-SCP-021's existing Ed25519 receipt mechanism (consult-class semantics). The markdown report is informational and CODEOWNERS-protected via `docs/scorecards/** @jrnb2024`; operators can hand-edit before merging the aggregator PR if needed (regenerator overwrites on next run). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the MCP server runs at acc.brokapps.ai; if down, agents lose `consult_scorecard` access but the markdown report + git index remain consultable. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation |\n    94\t| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
